WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.533
Claude Opus 5.5 took 31 minutes and spent
3 dollars weaving this physics cloth,

00:00:05.533 --> 00:00:09.267
and its own calculations dragged
it down to 28 frames per second.

00:00:09.300 --> 00:00:14.467
But Gemini 4 Argon delivered
it in 7 minutes, for 65 cents,

00:00:14.467 --> 00:00:18.383
running locked at 120 frames
per second with a laser scalpel.

00:00:18.383 --> 00:00:21.600
Six extreme challenges,
one single attempt,

00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:24.450
over two and a half hours
of autonomous coding,

00:00:24.450 --> 00:00:28.083
and a token bill that costs
nearly three times as much.

00:00:31.683 --> 00:00:32.583
I am Kevin, this is Mixiuh Labs,

00:00:32.583 --> 00:00:38.467
and today we put the two heavyweights
of AI programming into the laboratory.

00:00:38.500 --> 00:00:42.683
In the left corner, Claude Opus 5.5
from Anthropic, and on the right,

00:00:42.683 --> 00:00:46.833
Gemini 4 Argon from Google,
both running in High reasoning mode.

00:00:46.867 --> 00:00:48.900
The rules are unforgiving:

00:00:48.900 --> 00:00:53.933
the exact same prompt,
a single zero-shot attempt with no fixes,

00:00:53.933 --> 00:00:57.350
and zero external physics
engines or textures.

00:00:57.350 --> 00:01:01.533
And this time I didn't just measure
the stopwatch and frame rate:

00:01:01.533 --> 00:01:04.133
I also audited every input, output,

00:01:04.133 --> 00:01:08.250
and cached token to calculate
the exact dollar cost of each build.

00:01:08.283 --> 00:01:10.283
Because here we test with real data:

00:01:10.283 --> 00:01:15.500
six levels of pure math physics,
GPU shaders, and synthesized audio.

00:01:15.850 --> 00:01:20.550
Level 1: code from scratch a Verlet
integrator with a cloth that stretches,

00:01:20.550 --> 00:01:24.367
visualizes mechanical tension,
and tears in real time.

00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:27.733
Opus 5.5 took 31 minutes
and 18 tool steps,

00:01:27.733 --> 00:01:32.000
because on its own initiative it spun up
a headless browser to test its own code.

00:01:32.033 --> 00:01:37.533
Its cloth packs 3,200 particles
and over 18,000 structural, shear,

00:01:37.533 --> 00:01:41.883
and bend springs, but solving
30 iterations per frame on the CPU drags

00:01:41.883 --> 00:01:43.933
it down to 28 frames per second.

00:01:50.467 --> 00:01:56.800
Argon didn't hesitate: in just 2 steps
and 7 minutes it wrote 2,376 clean lines

00:01:56.800 --> 00:02:00.800
of code that run locked
at 120 frames per second.

00:02:00.833 --> 00:02:04.783
When you slice with the laser scalpel
or fire the radial shockwave in Argon,

00:02:04.783 --> 00:02:08.617
the tear propagates instantly
and the neon stress mode glows

00:02:08.617 --> 00:02:10.500
without dropping a single frame.

00:02:17.500 --> 00:02:18.550
And look at the bill:

00:02:18.550 --> 00:02:22.133
3 dollars and 5 cents for Opus
versus 65 cents for Argon.

00:02:22.133 --> 00:02:24.300
Four and a half times faster,
four times smoother,

00:02:24.300 --> 00:02:26.600
and nearly five times cheaper:
first point to Argon.

00:02:26.983 --> 00:02:31.767
Level 2: solve the Navier-Stokes equations
on the graphics card using GLSL shaders

00:02:31.767 --> 00:02:33.433
and vorticity confinement.

00:02:33.433 --> 00:02:40.033
Here Opus 5.5 took 18 minutes and built
a 20-buffer GPU pipeline featuring

00:02:40.033 --> 00:02:42.467
a textbook Karman wind tunnel.

00:02:42.500 --> 00:02:47.417
Its scientific velocity, vorticity,
and pressure visualization modes look

00:02:47.417 --> 00:02:50.967
super clean and hold a solid
120 frames per second.

00:02:57.500 --> 00:03:01.967
But Argon delivered in 10 minutes,
for 1 dollar and 40 cents,

00:03:01.967 --> 00:03:06.933
and on top of the Karman tunnel it added
a multi-pass GPU Bloom post-process

00:03:06.933 --> 00:03:09.383
that makes the bioluminescent dye glow.

00:03:09.417 --> 00:03:13.633
Plus, Argon lets you draw freehand solid
walls with your cursor right inside

00:03:13.633 --> 00:03:15.583
the flow to deflect vortices live.

00:03:22.617 --> 00:03:26.817
Both models mastered GPU shaders
at 120 frames per second,

00:03:26.817 --> 00:03:30.083
but the visual polish, interactive walls,

00:03:30.083 --> 00:03:33.300
and nearly half the time make
it two to zero for Argon.

00:03:33.683 --> 00:03:38.667
Level 3: 3D soft-body physics coupled
to a Raymarching engine with signed

00:03:38.667 --> 00:03:40.900
distance fields and subsurface scattering.

00:03:40.933 --> 00:03:42.850
This was Argon's heaviest challenge:

00:03:42.850 --> 00:03:49.433
it took 35 minutes to finish 2,759 lines
of code, though it held 120 frames per

00:03:49.433 --> 00:03:52.900
second and a dual view showing
both skeleton and skin.

00:03:59.433 --> 00:04:02.167
And here Opus 5.5 made a statement:

00:04:02.167 --> 00:04:06.033
for the only time in the entire tournament
it beat Argon on the clock,

00:04:06.033 --> 00:04:07.733
finishing in 31 minutes.

00:04:07.767 --> 00:04:13.150
Opus coded a tetrahedral XPBD solver
with volume conservation and six different

00:04:13.150 --> 00:04:16.283
shapes, including a star
and a rubber duck,

00:04:16.283 --> 00:04:18.633
over a floor with specular reflections.

00:04:26.167 --> 00:04:32.050
Even though Opus runs at 82 frames per
second and cost 3 dollars and 8 cents,

00:04:32.050 --> 00:04:37.183
its shape variety and tetrahedral
physics earn its first point: two to one.

00:04:37.533 --> 00:04:41.817
Level 4: a Minecraft-style
voxel world with 3D noise caves,

00:04:41.817 --> 00:04:45.967
Greedy Meshing optimization,
and per-vertex ambient occlusion.

00:04:47.017 --> 00:04:52.283
What Opus 5.5 built in 24 minutes
is wild in scale: it generated 213 chunks

00:04:52.283 --> 00:04:57.533
with over 2.5 million active voxels
running at 120 frames per second.

00:04:57.567 --> 00:05:01.817
Its snow-capped mountains feature
real ambient occlusion in every corner

00:05:01.817 --> 00:05:04.367
and flood-fill light
propagation for lanterns.

00:05:10.900 --> 00:05:14.550
But Argon answered
in just 7 minutes and 2 steps,

00:05:14.550 --> 00:05:19.333
costing only 67 cents compared
to 2 dollars and 51 cents for Opus.

00:05:19.333 --> 00:05:22.317
Argon's world includes complete
trees, translucent water,

00:05:22.317 --> 00:05:24.267
an automatic day and night cycle,

00:05:24.267 --> 00:05:29.250
and a hotbar with 3D isometric
block icons drawn purely by code.

00:05:36.283 --> 00:05:42.700
Opus won on raw geometry scale, but Argon
delivered a more complete world 3.4 times

00:05:42.700 --> 00:05:47.617
faster and nearly four times
cheaper: three to one for Argon.

00:05:48.000 --> 00:05:53.067
Level 5: a polyphonic frequency
modulation synthesizer in Web Audio API

00:05:53.067 --> 00:05:59.533
with a 16-step sequencer and FFT analyzer,
using zero external audio samples.

00:05:59.567 --> 00:06:04.883
Argon took 8 minutes and spent just 61
cents to deliver an 8-voice synthesizer

00:06:04.883 --> 00:06:09.617
with 4 musical demos and a split screen
showing both oscilloscope and spectrum.

00:06:20.150 --> 00:06:24.383
But when I opened Opus
5.5's 23-minute build,

00:06:24.383 --> 00:06:27.183
it looked like a professional
studio workstation.

00:06:27.217 --> 00:06:31.200
Opus built a four-operator DX7
engine with eight algorithms,

00:06:31.200 --> 00:06:34.633
a full effects rack with chorus,
delay, reverb, and master limiter,

00:06:34.633 --> 00:06:36.333
and four live visualizers:

00:06:36.333 --> 00:06:41.167
oscilloscope, FFT, stereo Lissajous
goniometer, and waterfall spectrogram.

00:06:53.200 --> 00:06:55.717
It cost 2 dollars and 66 cents,

00:06:55.717 --> 00:06:59.583
but that level of audio engineering
deserves the point without question:

00:06:59.583 --> 00:07:01.633
the score tightens to three to two.

00:07:02.017 --> 00:07:05.817
Final level: a Cyberpunk Pinball table
with continuous collision detection

00:07:05.817 --> 00:07:08.900
so the ball never tunnels through
the flippers at supersonic speed.

00:07:08.933 --> 00:07:13.233
Opus 5.5 took 32 minutes and designed
a sleek table with a classic arcade

00:07:13.233 --> 00:07:16.933
dot-matrix display and collision
vector visualization.

00:07:25.933 --> 00:07:27.433
Argon delivered
in 23 minutes for 97 cents,

00:07:27.433 --> 00:07:29.050
versus nearly 3 dollars for Opus,

00:07:29.050 --> 00:07:35.267
and pushed physics to the limit
with up to 28 sub-steps per frame:

00:07:35.267 --> 00:07:38.433
over 226,000 collision checks per second.

00:07:38.467 --> 00:07:43.267
And to prove its continuous collision
detection is indestructible,

00:07:43.267 --> 00:07:48.267
Argon added a triple Multiball stress test
button that fires three balls at brutal

00:07:48.267 --> 00:07:51.833
speed ricocheting across
bumpers with synthesized sound.

00:08:02.367 --> 00:08:07.550
With its debug overlay turned on you
can see the spatial grid, surface normals,

00:08:07.550 --> 00:08:11.050
and capsule sweeps running
at 120 frames per second:

00:08:11.050 --> 00:08:15.150
final point and a four
to two victory for Gemini 4 Argon.

00:08:15.533 --> 00:08:19.633
Four to two for Gemini 4 Argon
in our second Mixiuh Labs tournament,

00:08:19.633 --> 00:08:22.883
with all twelve projects working
on the very first attempt.

00:08:25.250 --> 00:08:30.200
On the total stopwatch, Argon completed
all six challenges in 90 minutes,

00:08:30.200 --> 00:08:36.383
an hour and a half, while Opus 5.5 needed
159 minutes, two hours and 39 minutes.

00:08:36.417 --> 00:08:38.667
Why did Opus take so long?

00:08:38.667 --> 00:08:44.467
Because in its agentic mode it executed
143 tool steps versus 45 for Argon,

00:08:44.467 --> 00:08:48.867
spinning up browser tests on its own
to inspect every build before finishing.

00:08:48.900 --> 00:08:51.250
And that hits your wallet directly:

00:08:51.250 --> 00:08:56.100
Opus consumed 13 million tokens
and cost 16 dollars and 26 cents,

00:08:56.100 --> 00:09:01.717
while Argon used 3.6 million tokens
and cost just 6 dollars and 26 cents.

00:09:03.183 --> 00:09:08.950
My takeaway is clear: Claude Opus 5.5
is a meticulous engineer that shines

00:09:08.950 --> 00:09:11.600
when a challenge demands
deep architecture,

00:09:11.600 --> 00:09:16.400
like the DX7 synthesizer
or tetrahedral soft bodies.

00:09:16.433 --> 00:09:20.917
But Gemini 4 Argon writes more working
code in nearly half the time,

00:09:20.917 --> 00:09:24.633
holds 120 frames per
second across all six levels,

00:09:24.633 --> 00:09:27.883
and costs 2.6 times less in tokens.

00:09:27.883 --> 00:09:32.267
I'm leaving all twelve playable projects
and the full benchmark log with prompts

00:09:32.267 --> 00:09:36.283
and token costs in the description
so you can test them yourself.

00:09:36.317 --> 00:09:41.350
Subscribe to Mixiuh Labs and tell me
in the comments which model showdown you

00:09:41.350 --> 00:09:43.233
want to see in the next experiment.

00:09:43.617 --> 00:09:45.800
Experiment 02 complete:

00:09:45.800 --> 00:09:49.883
see you in the next level,
right here on Mixiuh Labs.
